/** Retrofit batch AP -- upgrading old 2/3-case gpe-history problems to the 4-case standard
 * (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress). All solvers below were independently written and verified
 * byte-for-byte against each problem's real official Sample (or, where corrupted, against the
 * already-seeded boundary case) before being trusted to generate new tricky/stress expected output,
 * EXCEPT gpe-10038 whose official Sample is corrupted (see below).
 *
 * gpe-10038-disk-tree: build a trie from the backslash-split paths, then DFS-print with each depth
 *   indented by that many spaces, children sorted lexicographically, and a blank line after each
 *   TOP-LEVEL directory tree (matching both the problem's own "blank line after each dataset" rule
 *   and the fact that this trailing blank naturally separates top-level trees too). THE SCRAPED
 *   OFFICIAL SAMPLE IS MISSING ALL LEADING INDENTATION SPACES -- a scraper artifact; the solver was
 *   instead verified against the already-seeded (correctly indented) boundary test case, which
 *   matches exactly. sampleLimit: 0.
 * gpe-10416-last-digit: since last-digit(i^i) mod 10 is periodic in i with period 20 (a well-known
 *   result: last digit of a^b depends on a mod 10 and b mod 4-ish, and since i appears as both base
 *   and exponent here, the combined period is lcm(10,4)=20), split N (via BigInt, since N can reach
 *   2*10^100) into N=20*q+r, and sum = q*periodSum + partialSum(r), all reduced mod 10. Verified
 *   against the already-seeded boundary case's 100-digit N value.
 * gpe-10429-contest-scoreboard: direct submission-log simulation, first-correct-submission wins per
 *   problem (further submissions for an already-solved problem are ignored per the problem's own
 *   scoring rules), penalty = solve time + 20 per prior incorrect. A real bug caught here: the
 *   problem's own literal wording is "decreasing amounts of penalty time" for the tie-break -- i.e.
 *   HIGHER penalty ranks ABOVE lower penalty among contestants tied on problems solved, the reverse
 *   of standard ICPC convention -- confirmed by the already-seeded boundary case's own tie (5 owner
 *   with penalty 50 ranks above 7 with penalty 15, both having solved exactly 1 problem).
 * gpe-10432-mine-sweeper: direct board simulation; per the problem's own rule, touching a mine reveals
 *   ALL mines as asterisks (not just the touched one) and suppresses all number reveals. Verified
 *   against all 3 already-seeded test cases, including the touched-mine-loss case.
 * gpe-10468-maximum-product: classic running max/min product DP (needed because a negative running
 *   product can become the new maximum when multiplied by another negative value), clamped to 0 when
 *   no positive-product subarray exists, per the problem's own explicit rule. Verified against the
 *   already-seeded boundary case (a lone negative element correctly yielding 0).
 * gpe-10422-is-this-integration: derived an exact closed-form via circular-segment decomposition of
 *   the 4-quarter-circle overlap regions -- classifying by exact overlap MULTIPLICITY (confirmed via
 *   fine-grid numerical integration first): multiplicity-4 (center) = "striped", multiplicity-3 =
 *   "dotted", multiplicity-2 (minimum possible, no region is covered by fewer than 2 arcs) = "rest".
 *   Each area was derived exactly via inclusion-exclusion over pairwise/triple circle intersections
 *   (using standard lens-area formulas plus an explicit triangle+3-circular-segments decomposition of
 *   the triple-overlap region, whose vertices were found by direct algebraic circle-intersection
 *   solving): striped=a^2(1+pi/3-sqrt3), dotted=a^2(pi/3+2sqrt3-4), rest=a^2(4-2pi/3-sqrt3). Verified
 *   exactly (to all 3 decimal places) against the real sample's own 3 datasets and the already-seeded
 *   boundary case (a=1 -> 0.315 0.511 0.174).
 * gpe-10465-necklace: a "necklace" between S and T is exactly a chain of biconnected components (in
 *   the block-cut-tree sense) that are each SIMPLE CYCLES (including a 2-vertex cycle formed by a
 *   multi-edge, confirmed by the already-seeded boundary case), connected end-to-end through shared
 *   cut vertices -- solved via iterative (stack-safe, since N can reach 10000) Tarjan biconnected
 *   components using an edge-id stack (correctly distinguishing multi-edges by ID, not endpoint
 *   vertex), union-finding together all vertices of every component with edgeCount==vertexCount
 *   (the exact condition for "is a simple cycle"), then checking find(S)==find(T). Verified against
 *   the real sample's own 3 datasets.
 */
import { seedFromSample } from "./testcase-seed-helper.js";

